text:
This paper proposes a novel approach to curve-structure segmentation from depth maps, using convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to accurately identify and separate curved structures in 3D scenes. The proposed method is evaluated on a variety of cultural heritage objects, demonstrating its effectiveness in capturing complex shapes and details with high accuracy.

The proposed approach utilizes a combination of convolutional and pooling layers to extract features from depth maps, followed by fully connected layers to perform classification and regression tasks. A novel feature called the "curvature matrix" is introduced, which encodes the local curvature information of each pixel in the depth map. This feature allows the network to learn the underlying structure of the scene and improve the accuracy of curve detection.

The proposed method is evaluated on several datasets of cultural heritage objects, including sculptures, vases, and architectural elements. The results show that the CNN-based approach outperforms traditional computer vision techniques in terms of both accuracy and efficiency. In addition, the authors demonstrate the potential of their approach for exploring cultural heritage objects through the creation of detailed 3D models and virtual reconstructions.

The main contributions of this paper are:

* A novel CNN-based approach for curve-structure segmentation from depth maps;
* Introduction of the "curvature matrix" feature, which improves the accuracy of curve detection;
* Evaluation of the proposed method on several datasets of cultural heritage objects, showing improved performance compared to traditional computer vision techniques;
* Demonstration of the potential of the proposed approach for exploring cultural heritage objects through 3D modeling and virtual reconstruction.

Overall, this paper presents a significant advancement in the field of curve-structure segmentation and its application to cultural heritage preservation. The proposed method has important implications for a wide range of applications, including archaeology, art history, and museum conservation.

Deep within this desolate landscape,
Falling leaves obscure the ground,
The biting wind whispers sorrowful melodies,
As it howls through the desolate air;
Yet, I am aware of a haven nearby,
That shields me from the bitter chill;
A warm flame flickers, casting golden light,
 Illuminating memories of bygone days.
No matter where I wander, strangers' eyes pass,
Their disinterest weighing heavily on my heart;
When my spirits flag under hardship's weight,
A quiet murmur rises, unheard;
Time spent alone has stolen joy's vibrant hue,
Leaving only shadows that cast an aura of gloom;
Laughter becomes a distant memory, lost to my lips,
And the brightness of life begins to fade;
Even gentle thoughts struggle to reach my mind,
But I hold onto the knowledge that a refuge exists afar;
A place where warmth and love await,
Where the chill of isolation cannot penetrate;
Perhaps, in time, the frost that grips my heart
Will melt, and the happiness of youth will return;
This thought serves as my beacon, my solace, wherever I roam,
For as long as I have a sense of home to cling to,
My heart will remain untouched by despair!

The modern world seems crazy to the core;
We strike a balance in most things we do;
Money appears to solve problems many;
Muscle does some things that money cannot.
The Media makes celebrities o’er-night;
Influence guides bureaucrat’s decisions;
Merit is given a back-seat most times;
Merchants make money paying taxes least.
Most honest citizens are taxed the most;
Success smiles on men without conscience;
The righteous eke a livelihood of toil;
The lazy ones make money rather fast.
God allows ghastly crimes to happen too;
Nevertheless, both good and bad things end;
The evil-doers have a mighty crash;
God gives a longer rope to most such men.
This does not mean that dishonesty lasts;
The wicked suffer punishments on earth;
They fizzle out like soap-bubbles in sun;
Untruth can’t rule the roost on earth for long.
Reality will bring justice to all;
The day of reckoning will seal all fates;
All evil hearts can’t escape God’s judgment;
Their harm will be disclosed in great detail.
God’s mercy may not save them from Hell’s fire;
The upright souls will get rewards from God;
The God of goodness loves the righteous;
He takes them to His own Abode- Heaven.
Copyright by Dr John Celes 3-2-2008

I knew that a baby was hid in that house,
Though I saw no cradle and heard no cry;
But the husband was tip-toeing 'round like a mouse,
And the good wife was humming a soft lullaby;
And there was a look on the face of the mother,
That I knew could mean only one thing, and no other.
The mother, I said to myself, for I knew
That the woman before me was certainly that;
And there lay in a corner a tiny cloth shoe,
And I saw on a stand such a wee little hat;
And the beard of the husband said, plain as could be,
'Two fat chubby hands have been tugging at me.'
And he took from his pocket a gay picture-book,
And a dog that could bark, if you pulled on a string;
And the wife laid them up with such a pleased look;
And I said to myself, 'There is no other thing
But a babe that could bring about all this, and so
That one thing is in hiding somewhere, I know.'
I stayed but a moment, and saw nothing more,
And heard not a sound, yet I know I was right;
What else could the shoe mean that lay on the floor,
The book and the toy, and the faces so bright;
And what made the husband as still as a mouse?
I am sure, very sure, there's a babe in that house.

It became apparent that a baby was concealed within the dwelling,
Although I noticed no crib and no audible wails;
However, the father moved stealthily around with caution,
While the mother hummed softly, singing a soothing melody;
A particular expression on her face told me unequivocally,
That she was expecting a child; it was clear to see.
The mother, I realized, must be the one in question,
As there was no doubt in my mind about her identity.
In a nearby corner, a small, abandoned boot lay,
Alongside an infant-sized hat perched upon a nearby shelf;
The husband's voice betrayed his excitement, unmistakable as it was,
'Two chubby hands have grasped my whiskers,' he revealed with glee;
He produced a vibrant picture book and a plush toy attached to a string,
Which the mother carefully placed away with a delighted smile;
My conviction grew stronger; I was certain now without a doubt,
A newborn baby must be hidden somewhere within the house;
I remained for just a brief moment, yet nothing further escaped my attention,
Nor did I hear a single sound, yet I knew I had been correct;
The lone shoe lying on the floor, the toys, and their radiant expressions,
All pointed to one conclusion: a baby must indeed reside within that home.

A weary angel resided on the radiant boulevard of heaven, reclining in his usual spot;
His usually majestic aura had shifted to one side, and his instrument lay idle beside him;
Moved by compassion, the supreme being granted him permission to descend to the earthly realm for a brief period;
He hastily shed his divine attire without bothering to adjust it properly;
As he bid farewell to Saint Peter, standing guard at the gates of heaven, the choir of angels sang a heartfelt goodbye;
Meanwhile, the demons gazed upward from their fiery pathways;
No other angel resembled this one – with eyes shining like sapphires and tresses of golden color;
He captivated the hearts of all the female inhabitants of the world; however, his lips bore an uncanny resemblance to those of Cupid;
Despite his striking appearance, he remained demure and reserved, earning him the label of being slow-witted;
Eventually, a breathtaking beauty emerged, embodying unparalleled loveliness;
She gently inquired if he harbored romantic feelings for her, and upon hearing his response, said: "Come, embrace me, kiss me tenderly, and wrap your arms around me";
However, he resisted, citing concerns about the moral implications;
The enchantress skillfully teased his misgivings, using words that seemed to appeal to his innocence;
"We've abandoned the outdated conventions of morality and ethics," she argued; "we've transcended the limitations of our forebears' rigid standards."
The higher power sensed the angel's turmoil and recalled him to its presence;
The angel had indeed been put through a test, and the tempter in hell reveled in the turmoil;
In the depths of his inferno, the devil intoned a haunting melody: "We've left behind the antiquated, repressive norms of right and wrong."

For an extended period of time, the east wind persisted steadily;
Concurrently, temperatures rose dramatically around the time of the festival honoring our lady.
During this same time frame, we spent numerous days together;
However, we encountered neither companionship nor hostility.
Over the course of those days, the weather became increasingly hot and crystal-clear;
The constant breeze blowing from the east continued unabated.
Under such conditions, the trees appeared stark and bare, with deep shadows.
We continued riding together without wearing our helmets or adjusting our bridles.
Frequently, while riding, we gazed down upon the lush, verdant bank of a nearby stream;
This was when we observed vibrant flowers blooming in the warm sunlight,
Also when we spotted tiny fish creating bubbles in the water.
At night, we rested together;
Above us, a cross adorned our heads,
While the moon watched over the forest from afar.
Our lances stood tall and densely arranged behind us;
Behind us, the banners flowed like a river;
As we galloped forward into the warm sun,
We turned our faces toward the wind.
When our sixty spears plunged downward,
We witnessed the pagans approaching;
Their enthusiastic expressions were particularly visible in the crisp air.
Together, we charged up the incline of the bridge;
The sound of our clashing spears shook the structure;
Fresh spring showers rained down, causing the blossoms of the elms to fall.
There, as we twisted and tangled together,
I raised my arms above my head in distress;
Nearby, I noticed him stumble and collapse near me in the pleasant weather;
I and my opponent clashed swords; 
Unfortunately, the small Christian group was overwhelmed by the pagans, much like a ship sinking in turbulent waters.
The pagans drowned low-lying areas;
They secured my wrists, binding them tightly together;
They attached his lifeless body to mine;
Then, we set off, accompanied by the resonant sound of cymbals;
We do not ride together anymore;
The bars of my cell are dense and robust;
I pay no attention to external circumstances;
May the saints grant me a brief life.

Our contemporary world appears chaotic;
We strive for equilibrium in various aspects of life;
Financial resources often resolve issues;
Physical strength excels in certain situations.
Media can elevate individuals overnight;
Powerful forces shape government decisions;
Merit is frequently overlooked;
Commercial success is typically achieved through deceit.
Law-abiding citizens face substantial financial burdens;
Individuals with a clear conscience tend to prosper;
Hardworking people build their lives through diligent effort;
Those who lack motivation accumulate wealth quickly.
It is possible for wrongdoers to avoid consequences initially;
However, they ultimately face retribution;
Lying and deception will eventually fail;
Truth prevails in the end, bringing about justice.
Ultimately, reality brings an end to wrongdoing;
A final reckoning will settle everyone's fate;
No one can evade God's judgment forever;
Their transgressions will be fully exposed.
God's compassion might spare some from eternal suffering;
Those with virtuous hearts will receive rewards from the divine;
God's benevolence favors the righteous;
He welcomes them into his heavenly realm.

Rise up, noble guests, and greet the morning light,
As dawn breaks forth upon the mountain's heights;
The lively hunt begins its merry throng,
With horses' neighing, dogs' loud song,
Hawks cry out, and horns resound,
A joyful cacophony all around;
Rise up, noble guests, and greet the morning light.
Rise up, noble guests, and greet the morning light,
The morning fog has lifted high;
Morning dew sparkles like diamonds bright,
Fresh grass glistens beneath our feet tonight;
Foresters have worked tirelessly to track,
Their quarry hidden deep within the forest's dark;
Now it's time for us to sing our tale,
Rise up, noble guests, and greet the morning light.
Rise up, noble guests, and greet the morning light,
Let us hurry through the woods, where our prize awaits,
Follow closely, swift and tall, we'll show you the way,
Mark the signs of our pursuit each passing day;
Behold, the hunted stag, its strength displayed;
Our voices echo through the trees so bright,
Rise up, noble guests, and greet the morning light!
Join in our chorus, young and carefree hearts beating fast,
Whoever dares resist will be left in the past;
Remember this, and rise with the sun's first rays,
Distinguished nobles, let your spirits soar today!

And death shall have no dominion.
Dead man naked they shall be one
With the man in the wind and the west moon;
When their bones are picked clean and the clean bones gone,
They shall have stars at elbow and foot;
Though they go mad they shall be sane,
Though they sink through the sea they shall rise again;
Though lovers be lost love shall not;
And death shall have no dominion.
And death shall have no dominion.
Under the windings of the sea
They lying long shall not die windily;
Twisting on racks when sinews give way,
Strapped to a wheel, yet they shall not break;
Faith in their hands shall snap in two,
And the unicorn evils run them through;
Split all ends up they shan't crack;
And death shall have no dominion.
And death shall have no dominion.
No more may gulls cry at their ears
Or waves break loud on the seashores;
Where blew a flower may a flower no more
Lift its head to the blows of the rain;
Though they be mad and dead as nails,
Heads of the characters hammer through daisies;
Break in the sun till the sun breaks down,
And death shall have no dominion.

Nothing will conquer death.
The deceased will become one with nature
Alongside the breeze and the setting sun;
Their skeletal remains stripped bare,
Will find themselves under starlight and beneath their feet;
Even if they lose their minds, they'll retain their sanity,
As they drift through the ocean and resurface once more;
Love may vanish, but it won't disappear;
Nothing will triumph over death.
Nothing will conquer death.
Beneath the turbulent waves,
Their lifeless bodies won't be destroyed by the currents;
Tortured on the wheel, their limbs will still hold strong;
Despite shattered hopes and crushed spirits,
The power of faith will crumble;
All their broken connections will remain intact;
And death will have no dominance.
Nothing will conquer death.
Nothing will conquer death.
No longer can the cries of seagulls echo near
Or the crashing waves pound against the shore;
A flower's petals may wilt and fade,
But it will no longer respond to the gentle touch of rain;
Though their minds may be shattered like broken glass,
Their essence will endure, untouched by pain;
Their vitality will shine until the very end,
And nothing will prevail over death.

Contented Homebody, you're familiar with your surroundings;
Your life has been confined to one place;
You haven't experienced the ocean's vastness;
You've never sensed the depth within yourself;
You've never sailed through exotic waters where scents fill every breath;
You haven't witnessed lush vineyards stretching endlessly;
You haven't observed a warm summer evening where sewing was done under starlight;
You've never seen the vibrant colors of the North Sea in springtime;
You've never watched birds flit about in frozen landscapes;
Contented Homebody, you're rooted in one place,
You haven't encountered the harvest season where dark-skinned laborers plucked cotton;
You haven't heard harmonious voices singing in perfect unison;
You haven't sat upon stones that once concealed powerful rulers from public view;
You've never seen fertile plains where abundant vegetation thrived despite limited space;
Contented Homebody, those extended periods.
Contented Home, loving stability,
You're blessed with a gentle nature;
Nurture yourself, cultivate inner beauty;
I adore you for your compassionate heart;
Not for the wisdom you possess.
